From c95af7b78a1931455e6f93ce302d7f62245d92bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Pitkin <robpitkin@google.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:11:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 16/32] sensors: Isolate virtual sensor immunity to Device
 Service

Under features::kSensorPrivacyMitigations, hardware sensor streams are
suspended when a document is hidden or loses focus. Previously,
FrameSensorProviderProxy checked
WebContentsSensorProviderProxy::HasVirtualSensors() to exempt virtual
sensors created via DevTools and WPT test_driver. Because
HasVirtualSensors() checked whether any virtual sensor was active tab-
wide, creating a virtual sensor of one type (e.g. ambient light) allowed
all physical hardware sensors in that tab (e.g. accelerometer,
gyroscope) to bypass suspension in background or unfocused frames.

This change separates frame lifecycle supervision from synthetic sensor
immunity:

1. In content/browser, FrameSensorProviderProxy reverts to a pure frame
lifecycle and focus supervisor. It unconditionally suspends all
SensorClientController connections when a frame loses focus or
visibility, with no awareness of virtual sensors or sensor types.

2. In services/device/generic_sensor, SensorImpl::IsSuspended() ignores
controller_suspended_ if sensor_->IsVirtual() returns true. Virtual
sensors remain immune to supervisory background suspension so test
harnesses and DevTools emulation do not freeze in background tabs, while
physical PlatformSensor instances stop hardware polling and clear their
shared memory buffers. Client-side suspension
(SensorImpl::client_suspended_) remains enforced for both virtual and
physical sensors per spec.

3. PlatformSensorFusion::IsVirtual() dynamically verifies that all
underlying source sensors are virtual, allowing DevTools orientation
emulation to inherit virtual immunity without manual type aliasing in
the browser.

